INT. RIVER ROCK QUARRY - FIRST-AID TENT - DAY

A canvas tent pitched at the lip of a limestone quarry. Dust drifts through the open flap. A stretcher bed, a cooler of saline bags, a battery lantern.

DR. SADIE TREMBLAY, forties, crouches over a pale quarryman, ARTUR NOWAK, who lies on the stretcher clutching his thigh. A tourniquet is tight above the knee. A young medic, JUNE ABERNATHY, kneels at his side with a roll of gauze.

She is shaking. Dr. Tremblay takes the gauze from her without a word and presses it to the wound. Artur howls through his teeth.

DR. TREMBLAY
Pressure. Hold it right there.

JUNE
He's losing it. He's going grey.

He grabs for June's wrist and she grabs his. They hold each other's grip.

ARTUR
Don't let me go.

JUNE
(over him)
I've got you.

DR. TREMBLAY
Artur, look at me.

ARTUR ^
I can't feel my foot.

Dr. Tremblay cuts the trouser leg with scissors. She reads the wound, and the tension in her shoulders eases by an inch.

DR. TREMBLAY
It missed the artery. Tourniquet did its job. June, loosen it a quarter turn.

June turns the windlass, her fingers steadying as she works. Artur lets his head fall back.

ARTUR
Is it bad?

DR. TREMBLAY
It's a deep gash. It's going to hurt for weeks and you're going to keep the leg.

He closes his eyes and laughs, a cracked sound. June sits back on her heels and wipes her face with the back of a bloody glove.
